```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a misconfigured default gateway on laptop0, not a downed
router0 sub-interface. Router0's VLAN-20 sub-interface eth0.20 is fully
operational (state UP, 172.16.20.1/24 assigned, reachable from laptop0 with
0% packet loss). The real problem is that laptop0's routing table carries the
default route "via 172.16.20.99 dev eth0" — 172.16.20.99 is a non-existent
address with no device assigned to it anywhere in the topology.

Because 172.16.20.99 does not exist, any packet laptop0 tries to send outside
172.16.20.0/24 triggers an ARP request for 172.16.20.99 that is never answered,
producing "Destination Host Unreachable" immediately. Intra-VLAN traffic is
unaffected because hosts in the same /24 are reached directly via ARP without
consulting the default route — consistent with the report that laptop0 still
reaches other VLAN-20 hosts.

The fault is localized entirely to laptop0's startup configuration. The bad
gateway was injected there (as evidenced by the kernel route table showing
"172.16.20.99" rather than the correct "172.16.20.1"). No other VLAN-20 or
VLAN-10 device is affected; laptop2 on VLAN 10 correctly uses 172.16.10.1 and
routes normally.

Root cause: laptop0's default gateway was set to 172.16.20.99 (a phantom
address) instead of the correct VLAN-20 gateway 172.16.20.1 (router0 eth0.20).

Corrective fix: On laptop0, delete the incorrect route
("ip route del default via 172.16.20.99") and add the correct one
("ip route add default via 172.16.20.1 dev eth0"), then make the correction
permanent in laptop0's startup script.
=== END ===
